# The words a message between two sessions is spelled with — the bash twin.
# SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH (F5 pattern, like gates.env), but the OWNER is
# viewer/shared/message-model.js and this file is its bash half: sourced by
# scripts/phase-graph.sh (--messaging, --notes) and by scripts/phase-msg.sh.
# viewer/test/gates-vocab.test.ts asks bash for every list below and holds it
# to the JS owner word for word, so the two can never disagree. Change BOTH.
#
# Plain KEY="VALUE" (sourced by bash 3.2) — space-separated tokens; the two
# caps are bare integers, like scripts/mcp.env.

#   MESSAGE_KINDS  — note (expects nothing back) · ask (wants an answer) ·
#                    reply (answers an ask, carrying its id). Three and not
#                    two: a note silently treated as a question is a session
#                    waiting on a peer nobody told to answer.
MESSAGE_KINDS="note ask reply"

#   MESSAGE_SCHEMES — the half of an address before the colon. `session` names
#                     a process; `phase` a phase of this plan, whoever runs it;
#                     `next` the phase that runs after this one, whenever that
#                     is; `all` every live session of the run; `run` the run
#                     itself; `repo` every session in one repository across
#                     plans; `operator` a person.
MESSAGE_SCHEMES="session phase next all run repo operator"

#   MESSAGE_DELIVER — WHEN the sender wants it to arrive; a request, not a
#                     promise. `boot` is the only way to address a phase that
#                     has not started.
MESSAGE_DELIVER="now next-turn boot"
#   MESSAGE_PRIORITIES — the RUN-admission vocabulary, worst-first, reused by
#                     identity rather than respelled: "how urgent is this" has
#                     one answer in this system (viewer/shared/orchestration-model.js).
MESSAGE_PRIORITIES="high normal low"

#   MESSAGE_STATES  — the delivery state machine, in order. `delivered` and
#                     `acked` are two facts: the CLI's inbox writes nothing
#                     back on any outcome (phase 1, arm S-B), so "the bytes
#                     went in" is all a sender learns from the connection.
MESSAGE_STATES="queued held delivering delivered acked expired refused failed"

#   MESSAGE_VIAS    — the transport that actually carried it: a fact about the
#                     past, which is why it is a different list from
#                     MESSAGE_DELIVER, a wish about the future.
MESSAGE_VIAS="socket stdin boot-prompt inbox"

#   MESSAGE_REFUSALS — why it was refused. Each is a different repair, which is
#                      the only reason to enumerate rather than carry a sentence.
MESSAGE_REFUSALS="no-recipient not-live over-budget too-large self-addressed unknown-scheme duplicate"

#   MESSAGING_WORDS / DEFAULT_MESSAGING — the plan-wide `**Messaging:**` line.
#                      On unless a plan turns it off: it costs nothing unused.
MESSAGING_WORDS="on off"
DEFAULT_MESSAGING="on"

#   MESSAGE_MAX_BYTES     — 8 KB. A message is a sentence a person could have
#                           said; anything longer is a file, and a file belongs
#                           in the repository the recipient can read at its own
#                           pace rather than in a prompt it pays for every turn.
#   MESSAGE_MAX_PER_PHASE — 16 sends. Generous for a handful of notes, small
#                           enough that a loop cannot drain an account: a
#                           session that hits it is not chatting, it is stuck.
MESSAGE_MAX_BYTES=8192
MESSAGE_MAX_PER_PHASE=16
